WEBVTT

1
00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:29.770
Katariina Forsberg: Hello.

2
00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:30.730
Art A: Hello, cat.

3
00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:32.840
Katariina Forsberg: We're here.

4
00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.220
Art A: Amazing. How you doing?

5
00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:37.680
Katariina Forsberg: Doing alright.

6
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:45.779
Art A: Good. I think this is going to be recorded as well, so… it may just be you and me, maybe a group of people, we'll see.

7
00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.030
Katariina Forsberg: I'll see what happens in the next hour.

8
00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.599
Katariina Forsberg: Yeah, it would feel better if nobody joined, but…

9
00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:00.329
Katariina Forsberg: It's okay if they join, I know that's the whole purpose. I mean, there's recording, so…

10
00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:11.869
Art A: There's a recording, so please feel free to review the recording if you're listening to this. I actually spoke to someone yesterday who said that he's been listening to all of the YouTubes, and I was like, oh, wow.

11
00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:13.880
Katariina Forsberg: Well, that's good.

12
00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:14.580
Art A: Right.

13
00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:18.670
Katariina Forsberg: I do see views on it, so people are viewing it.

14
00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:32.859
Art A: It's interesting, because this is a really… this particular slot is quite challenging for folks in Asia, because it's 7am in Singapore and Hong Kong right now. It's 9am my time, which is okay, and it's, like, 5pm your time?

15
00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:34.890
Katariina Forsberg: Yeah, 4 p.m.

16
00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:48.820
Art A: Okay, yeah, I think it's fine. We'll wait a couple more minutes and see if anyone hops on, otherwise we can just dive in and you and I can have a bit of a powwow. This can be a really good, actually, working session for us to work through some of the feedback that we're getting.

17
00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:49.600
Katariina Forsberg: Yeah.

18
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:55.780
Art A: And we can see if we're… We're online.

19
00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:58.270
Art A: Just wait to see.

20
00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:53.840
Art A: Give them another minute, and then we can rush into some of the things. Thanks, Kat.

21
00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.999
Art A: Okay, it's 9.02. Let's go, let's kick it off.

22
00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:16.459
Katariina Forsberg: Dark, okay.

23
00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:17.820
Art A: Go.

24
00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:33.569
Katariina Forsberg: I can go. Welcome to the first fortnightly PDPP Community Call. Public comments on the standard close on October 1st, which is a week from today. Today is about governance, how the program around the standard is run.

25
00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:45.539
Katariina Forsberg: And technical comments are for our next call on October 8th with Tim, and you can comment anytime on GitHub or on the PDP Connect Discuss mailing list.

26
00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:53.839
Katariina Forsberg: So, Art, to start us off, what has changed in the governance draft since we last met as a group?

27
00:08:54.740 --> 00:09:06.820
Art A: So, we obviously met at GDC with some of the folks in the community live, so I think some of them will be listening to this recording as well. Hello. We wanted to,

28
00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:14.960
Art A: For governance, we kind of split it into two different phases. We have kind of, like, the operating rules being enforced.

29
00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:23.110
Art A: Which is the purpose of this public community review. And then we have, the proposed structure,

30
00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:31.969
Art A: For future governance and long-term governance. We also made sure that we looked at how conformance is measured, so we had…

31
00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:38.210
Art A: Kind of, folks that could sign up to say,

32
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:46.140
Art A: I agree with the general principles of PDPP, which is, you know, setting an intention.

33
00:09:46.140 --> 00:10:02.629
Art A: And then people who are actually conformant with PDPP. And then, there's self-checks, which is verified by software packages that we're going to be creating and putting in the GitHub repo. And then, for things that require discretion.

34
00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:15.460
Art A: this is all part of the governance, but we're thinking about an interim technical committee, as well as a longer-term technical committee for that structure, for what we call verified status. So, conformance is,

35
00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:26.000
Art A: is self-check and then verified, and then there's also a different status, which is saying, like, I actually just, I support this stat… I support PDPP as a project.

36
00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:37.220
Katariina Forsberg: Got it. And the standard now sits with the Linux Foundation, and people have asked what the legal body will hold the program as it grows.

37
00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.899
Katariina Forsberg: Art, where does the program live sit?

38
00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:42.080
Katariina Forsberg: And where's it headed?

39
00:10:42.080 --> 00:11:01.339
Art A: Yeah, that's a… that was an interesting question. So, I think we're getting into the meat now of, like, what, what some of the feedback was in relation to governance, and so, there were folks that raised questions around what is the legal structure of the entity that would be granting conformance standards to different aspects of the system.

40
00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.469
Art A: I think,

41
00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:15.010
Art A: there were a couple of options that we looked at. So we looked at whether… so, so just for, just for, kind of.

42
00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:25.289
Art A: situational awareness here. This currently sits as PDP Connect is a lab within LFDT.

43
00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:35.240
Art A: And, this is building an open source, community specification out of PDPP, which is… sorry, out of PDP Connect, which is PDPP.

44
00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:38.270
Art A: And, I think…

45
00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:54.260
Art A: there are different views as to how we should… or when we should even define what the legal entity is that's able to award the conformance to different aspects of the system. One view is that it should…

46
00:11:54.350 --> 00:12:00.210
Art A: be settled now. One view, or before we do anything further.

47
00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:10.970
Art A: One view is that the current phases, which are the interim phases of applying the PDPP standard, should just go ahead, and then,

48
00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:22.099
Art A: And the other view is that, the elected committee, once we've done that, should then choose. So I think options 2 and 3 kind of go hand in hand. So, like, we do a period of…

49
00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:35.069
Art A: Not sure what the legal structure here should be, but it should be community-driven, so whomever is elected as part of the community should be the ones who are saying, yes, this is the standard, this is the legal body that we want, and then deal with the Linux Foundation there.

50
00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:43.630
Art A: we've kind of… so, the core contributors, myself, Kat, Tim, and Anna, have gone back to LFGT and asked for advice on this, and I think…

51
00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:56.420
Art A: there is a call coming up, but we're actually discussing this with them, so, that's one thing to think about. But I think where I'm landing on this, and what I would recommend, as one of the stewards here, is that we…

52
00:12:56.650 --> 00:13:10.119
Art A: keep the lab structure in the interim phases, and then work out the right Linux Foundation home, before we progress to a formal membership and formal,

53
00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:14.639
Art A: Kind of, technical steering committee style structure.

54
00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.490
Art A: And so what that means is, in the interim.

55
00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:26.750
Art A: People can be given conformance, certification through the existing lab, PDP Connect.

56
00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:39.090
Art A: And that, there will be some sort of discussion around incorporation and what that looks like, maybe Q1 next year, because… and the rationale there is, like, we gotta get going on this, like…

57
00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:47.489
Art A: There's already calls for national implementations of PDPP, and it's like, we just need to get something out there, and to be able to certify folks as conformant.

58
00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:54.449
Art A: And so, I would propose that that's the pro- that's the kind of way we go.

59
00:13:54.560 --> 00:14:04.199
Art A: Obviously, this is going to be subject to community feedback, so, anybody who's listening to the video, please feel free to, offer your thoughts.

60
00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.149
Art A: What's next, Kat?

61
00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:18.110
Katariina Forsberg: So membership is free now, but people have asked, who could sign a contract or accept funding for the program. Who would sign if someone offered support tomorrow?

62
00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:24.440
Art A: I mean, I think because we sit as an LFDT lab right now,

63
00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:42.410
Art A: I would say that any… anybody who's willing to commit money should do so at the LFDT level, because we don't have a legal entity to be able to hold funds right now. And that's why we kind of designed it so that the program is voluntary in its first iteration, and…

64
00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:50.690
Art A: The issue that comes up here, which is probably stickier, is signing authority. So, to the extent that signing authority is a… is a concern for…

65
00:14:50.690 --> 00:15:09.009
Art A: anything that the lab needs to do, I think we need to go defer back to LFDT and go back to Daniela and say, like, hey, there's this thing that we need to do, we probably need to take the legal personality from there, and that could, end up, like, catalyzing a discussion around legal entities related to, PDPP or PDP Connect.

66
00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:19.040
Art A: So, I would propose that there are no fees in the first iteration, and we would redirect any financial commitments to LFDT. Keep it clean.

67
00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:35.719
Art A: And, any legal obligations go through, LFDT as well, so that the lab is, like, doesn't have a legal personality, until the steering committee is seated, and until that steering committee has, decided on

68
00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:40.830
Art A: what the governance structure should be for, PDP and PDP Connect.

69
00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:42.290
Katariina Forsberg: Yep.

70
00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:55.680
Katariina Forsberg: A few people have asked whether regulators would see the structure as open enough, and how a government could take part. Where do governments and regulators fit here?

71
00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:59.900
Art A: I think that's a really good call, and I think that that's where,

72
00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:16.769
Art A: In designing some of the governance, we put in supporter status. Now, the question is whether folks who are… like, question… people who are supporters… so, if you go to PDP… PDPP.dev, there's a section there where people can indicate their support of PDPP, and they can be involved in a lot of the work that we do.

73
00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:27.470
Art A: Now… my… My take on this is that…

74
00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:31.970
Art A: Governments or anyone, any individual or entity, should be able to indicate support.

75
00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:35.890
Art A: In the current version of governance.

76
00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:40.910
Art A: Just because you indicate support does not give you the right to be able to vote for who's on the steering committee.

77
00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:47.459
Art A: And I think that's where we can maybe think about exceptions for government here, where they could sit on the steering committee.

78
00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:53.400
Art A: Or have some voting rights associated with being a government.

79
00:16:53.650 --> 00:17:02.900
Art A: But I would say for simplicity's sake, the best thing to do now is…

80
00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:12.449
Art A: to allow governments to be supporters, and to indicate their support. When you indicate support, you can obviously use, you know, say that you indicate support for PDPP.

81
00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:28.529
Art A: And then later on, once the steering committee is constituted, create rules for how public bodies and other certifying organizations, NGOs, INGOs, and governments can be part of the membership, even if they can't implement any part of the standard.

82
00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:35.090
Art A: Because I think that that's really the interesting thing here, like, I don't see, say, you know, the government of Australia

83
00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:44.579
Art A: implementing a data source, a data connector, or a data operator. That's not to say that they can't, I just don't see that as being the normal course of work that they do.

84
00:17:44.580 --> 00:18:02.899
Art A: But they should be able to participate in this if it's to be a global standard, and so I wouldn't be opposed to that. I just think the rules there are too detailed for us to be doing now. So that would be what my proposal is, and again, if anybody who's listening to this has any feedback on it, I would really appreciate that.

85
00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:07.150
Art A: Yeah, I'm optimizing for simplicity and speed in the short term.

86
00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:21.919
Katariina Forsberg: Okay. So anyone can sign on as a supporter. Some people have asked whether that's just too easy for big companies. Art, what does being a supporter actually mean?

87
00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:38.910
Art A: That's a really good question, and I think that part of it was that we need to get people's eyes on this as quickly as possible, and around a common set of principles that should guide the way that data moves. So, we've seen rules entrenched in law but not implemented.

88
00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:44.770
Art A: But I don't think we even have common understanding of the principles of what it means to have

89
00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:49.540
Art A: human-centric or people-first data portability. So,

90
00:18:50.060 --> 00:19:02.379
Art A: the… you know, when we were drafting the governance requirements around what the PDPP principles were, we really tried to optimize for the most… the broadest, most general statement of what people

91
00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:12.599
Art A: who could be aligned with the standard could believe in as it relates to personal data moving. Now, is it too easy because it doesn't require a compliance? I would say two things to this.

92
00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:17.800
Art A: There's… one perspective is, yes, it's too easy, and we should get people to actually do some implementation.

93
00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:29.730
Art A: I think that because PDPP is built for people by people, we need to find a way for people to indicate that they personally support this idea of a user-owned internet and user-controlled data.

94
00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:44.140
Art A: Where they're not necessarily builders, they're just everyday people who are just like, hey, this is a good idea. And I think what that does is it shows that we have almost broader democratic support or, you know, community consensus that this is something that we want, even if you can't technically implement it.

95
00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:47.240
Art A: So that's kind of why the bar is low there.

96
00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:52.570
Art A: I think that to gate it by doing some sort of technical action.

97
00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:57.920
Art A: Could be reducing the base of supporters that would actually care about this.

98
00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:03.109
Art A: And I think that… but then where the rubber meets the road is how that relates to companies.

99
00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:16.219
Art A: So, should we have a different threshold for companies subscribing to the standard? I would say no, and the reason is because if a company is indicating their support of this, they should be invited and,

100
00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:20.040
Art A: They should be encouraged to participate.

101
00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:33.539
Art A: And you don't do that by saying you've got to do XYZ in order to be part of the club. You almost say, okay, so where are you at? Let's try to bring you along on this journey. So, I think a statement of intent is more than enough.

102
00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:40.779
Art A: And that's what I'm gonna propose here to the community, that a public statement of intent, and not necessarily a commitment to implement

103
00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:47.699
Art A: at the first stage is enough to indicate support and to be registered within the GitHub repo as being a supporter of PDPP.

104
00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:48.800
Art A: And then…

105
00:20:49.010 --> 00:21:02.519
Art A: that doesn't give people voting rights, so we gate, gate the ability to govern the standard to people who've actually implemented it and know what they're talking about. So, that's kind of the proposal that I would put forward in relation to whether supporters are too light.

106
00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:03.870
Katariina Forsberg: Yeah.

107
00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:14.780
Katariina Forsberg: And a big question has been why large platforms would take part at all. Art, what is it? What's in it for a platform?

108
00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:34.970
Art A: Yeah, interestingly, we had a couple of comments on this one, so, one via external review, and some via direct notes, in relation to, large platform support of this. I think, and this is more about, like, the go-to-market positioning of PDPP,

109
00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:41.430
Art A: I think it will definitely capture the support of individuals and organizations that are working in this space. I don't think that's a…

110
00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:50.319
Art A: that's gonna be challenging, although we do need to do some work there. Actually, if there's anybody in the community here who's interested in helping us with that work, please feel free to raise your hand and email cat or me.

111
00:21:51.900 --> 00:22:09.020
Art A: So I think reaching out to organizations and saying, hey, we're building this, that's, like, I think that that's… that's done. Well, that's easy. The smaller to middle-tier companies, I'm thinking, like, seed to Series B style companies that are still not large enough.

112
00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:14.630
Art A: And that see the lack of flow of data as being a problem to their business.

113
00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:31.100
Art A: They're also going to be fairly easy to talk to. Those are the folks that we work with most on Varna. So, I think our clientele would be very open to signing up for this, and saying that they're committing to this, at the very least, at the commitment, or,

114
00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:35.120
Art A: You know, saying that they align with support for PDPP.

115
00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:44.520
Art A: I think the large tech enterprises, and we were, we've been, fortunately helped by, Lisa at the Data Transfer Initiative.

116
00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.280
Art A: in relation to this.

117
00:22:47.740 --> 00:23:00.030
Art A: And she's provided some valuable insights from her experience. And it is still that some of these large platforms are very sensitive about the data that they carry, they see it as part of their technical moat.

118
00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:02.969
Art A: And I think we need to…

119
00:23:03.190 --> 00:23:06.970
Art A: go softly, softly with them. I actually don't think…

120
00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:13.129
Art A: That there's much that we can do other than show that there's groundswell… a groundswell of support.

121
00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.169
Art A: And I think…

122
00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:24.130
Art A: We need to, therefore, focus our attention on signing up as many individuals or smaller companies as possible.

123
00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:44.170
Art A: to show who's on what side, until we get to the point that we can have a conversation with the folks at larger companies and say, are you on the right side of things? And I think GDC was really good for that, because we were able to speak to companies of different sizes, and I spoke to some very large companies that were like, actually, ideologically, we do believe in this. So,

124
00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:49.310
Art A: My proposal to the community here is focus in on small and individuals first.

125
00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:51.770
Art A: Let's focus in on people who are already in our camp.

126
00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:56.629
Art A: Let's try to bring them into the… breathe them into the house, and then we can…

127
00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:04.649
Art A: Almost create the sides of history and offer to folks that they need to be on the right side of history on this one.

128
00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:15.689
Katariina Forsberg: The draft proposes a five-seat steering committee with one vote per organization. Art, why that design?

129
00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:24.979
Art A: Yeah, this was… this was raised in question 4, sorry, session 4. The design itself is, like…

130
00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:40.659
Art A: You know, we… I was debating 3 or 5, and candidly, I was the one who chose 5. 3 or 5, and I am anticipating that when we get the steering committee up and running, we… one of the requirements is we have more than 100 people who have signed up to the standard.

131
00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:42.669
Art A: And have implemented it.

132
00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:49.989
Art A: I think having 3 people would limit the choices of who these organizations might want to take this forward.

133
00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:55.139
Art A: The… I wanted to go with 5 for that reason.

134
00:24:55.460 --> 00:25:06.749
Art A: The other… the other thing is that I want it to be one vote per organization, so that, you know, as we talked about before, larger organizations tend to have a disproportionate say in how the,

135
00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:10.289
Art A: Architectures or infrastructures of technology actually work in the world.

136
00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:26.959
Art A: And I wanted to make sure that small voices can be amplified. So, if you are a company who is running PDPP conformant architecture, you should have just as much say in how the standard develops as, you know, one of the big tech companies. So,

137
00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:43.849
Art A: it was to ensure proportionate representation by company, because we all should have a say in this, and to balance that representation, and I wanted there to be enough seats where people felt like they had a representative that they could back in relation to this.

138
00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:57.659
Art A: I think that the voting mechanics do need to be sorted out. Like, is it preferential voting? Is it first past the post? I think, probably preferential voting, and then the top 5 after preferences.

139
00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:06.689
Art A: other ones that move forward. But, I think that's something that we need to design once we're closer to the point where we have a formal membership and we can kind of get to that point.

140
00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:08.420
Art A: That would be my proposal.

141
00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:09.490
Katariina Forsberg: Cool, yeah.

142
00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:12.689
Katariina Forsberg: One reviewer asked, what stops

143
00:26:12.890 --> 00:26:19.550
Katariina Forsberg: any one person or group holding too much power. What are the checks?

144
00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:24.999
Art A: Yeah, this is a comment by an external review as well, so,

145
00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:45.089
Art A: there's nothing specifying that the board or chair can't be on the technical committee. I would take this recommendation, because the recommendation was making it split, so allowing… so making sure that nobody who sits on the steering committee is able to be on the technical committee, and vice versa, just for…

146
00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:46.590
Art A: Just to remind folks.

147
00:26:46.590 --> 00:27:10.369
Art A: The steering committee is the one that manages the standard, so they're the ones that will review or take any feedback relating to the standard, develop the standard, and then put it out for a community vote, and they themselves will vote on different changes to the standard. These will need to be spun up in bylaws as to what the steering committee does, but their general rule… their general rule is they are the custodians of the standard.

148
00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:21.000
Art A: They appoint a technical committee, and that technical committee are the administrators of the standard. So they're the ones that ask, is this company conformant with this requirement in the standard that's been set?

149
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:32.599
Art A: The technical committee cannot change the standard unilaterally, that needs to go through the steering committee, and the steering committee can't dictate what the technical company does… technical committee does in terms of administration of the standard.

150
00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:50.719
Art A: So it creates that check that's really important, and I think one thing that we did miss in drafting, and I do… I will agree with this, is that, there needs to be separation of powers there, where the steering committee cannot… members of the steering committee cannot also serve on the technical committee once we spin up full membership.

151
00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:57.719
Art A: The other suggestion was removal of the chair, and I think that,

152
00:27:57.820 --> 00:28:08.769
Art A: to the extent that anyone on the steering committee needs to be removed, there does need to be a removal function, because we don't want them serving the term. I believe it's a one-year term.

153
00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:12.630
Art A: If there's something that, you know, happens in that term, so…

154
00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:30.659
Art A: My suggestion here, and this is what I will propose to the community, is that by simple majority, of partners, we do have… so, simple majority of the membership, which are partners, there is the power to remove anyone on either the steering committee or the technical committee, including the chair.

155
00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:36.520
Art A: And, I think… That is…

156
00:28:36.940 --> 00:28:45.990
Art A: that's kind of, that kind of resolution can be put forward by any member. That's what I'd suggest here. Again, open to thoughts on this,

157
00:28:46.250 --> 00:29:03.289
Art A: I mean, this is the stuff that comes up in hypothetical, but I know that some organizations, some labs, some projects have encountered issues like this before, so I think it's worthwhile mentioning it. But yeah, the right to remove should be there, and a separation of powers between the steering committee and the technical committee should exist.

158
00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:14.579
Katariina Forsberg: Now, when someone says their product meets the standard, the technical committee reviews it. Who makes the final call, and why?

159
00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:20.220
Art A: That's a good question, and this is where I've been… Going back and forth.

160
00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:22.090
Art A: So…

161
00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:39.700
Art A: This also kind of relates to the structure of governance, or the structure of legal personality that we have within PDP Connect. If we become an entity of itself, or some sort of entity that carries a level of legal personality and financial autonomy, legally there needs to be some sort of

162
00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:50.159
Art A: mechanism, whether it's a delegated mechanism for that body to say, you meet the standard here, you can use the IP, your license to be able to do this.

163
00:29:50.750 --> 00:29:59.069
Art A: I think that that legal step of saying, this is right, the responsibility of that should be within the

164
00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:08.979
Art A: steering committee, or the chair themselves. The chair as the legal, authority to be able to bind, the folks.

165
00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:18.729
Art A: The technical committee needs to be selected for their independence and their ability… and their technical rigor, hence the name.

166
00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:26.650
Art A: And so, they need to be able to independently assess something separate from political influence that would exist at the

167
00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:45.849
Art A: at the steering committee level. Obviously, there's appointments, right? So, you know, it's a majority appointment, but the individual on the technical committee needs to be somewhat independent. So, they need to be able to make an independent assessment. That would turn into a recommendation that

168
00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:51.700
Art A: The steering committee would need to adopt.

169
00:30:52.270 --> 00:31:11.479
Art A: so it's recommend, then grant. I think it needs to be a mandatory grant, with the exception of… and maybe we can do something like, unless a supermajority of the steering committee disagrees with the technical assessment, so in… it would be 4 out of 5.

170
00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:13.480
Art A: Then…

171
00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:24.930
Art A: the grant needs… the recommendation needs to go through. So, I think the steering committee can overrule the technical committee in the event of a supermajority.

172
00:31:25.090 --> 00:31:43.460
Art A: But the technical committee then maintains any other powers relating to certifying, verifying anyone as being conformant with PDPP. I think that strikes the right balance here. But again, open to thoughts from folks. This was something raised, so it's definitely, contentious.

173
00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:53.199
Katariina Forsberg: People have asked what happens after a company gets a status, or how do we make sure they keep their premises?

174
00:31:55.430 --> 00:32:00.450
Art A: That's a good call. I think… hmm…

175
00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:07.530
Art A: We have implemented annual compliance review and attestation,

176
00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:15.960
Art A: On the basis that the meeting of the rules at entry doesn't mean that you're continuing to meet them. There's a question of whether annual is enough.

177
00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:31.939
Art A: And I want to balance the burden that would be placed on the technical committee to do continuous monitoring versus, the security that we need in order to make sure that standards… that the standard is, conformant.

178
00:32:32.060 --> 00:32:56.860
Art A: And safe. Safe to use. So, what I'd actually propose here is that… so the key in PDPP, the key actor here is the operator. And I would suggest that the operator needs to make some sort of commitment to the community or to PDP Connect in some sort of contractual way, that they will maintain conformance and compliance with PDPP

179
00:32:56.860 --> 00:33:08.339
Art A: PDPP, otherwise they're not allowed to say that they're PDPP conformant, and they do… they need to have active obligations to notify PDP Connect that they are… that when they

180
00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:26.649
Art A: fall out of conformance, so… or whenever there's a risk to conformance, so a breach, or a risk, or some security issue, they do need to have active obligations, and that's only the operator. I also think that the steering… the technical committee should conduct

181
00:33:26.770 --> 00:33:33.440
Art A: For operators, quarterly reviews, not annual reviews. For everyone else, it's an annual review on membership.

182
00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:52.119
Art A: That should be, for me, the right balance between ensuring that the people who say they're conformant with the standard keep their promises, and not overburdening what is effectively a voluntary technical committee at this stage. And I think that

183
00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:57.460
Art A: So that deals with, like, the usual course of affairs, and then what happens when there is a…

184
00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:16.419
Art A: you know, an issue that comes up ad hoc. I think that anybody, and not just operators who, detect or see an issue in relation to the thing that they've been certified or verified for, they should have the obligation to inform PDP Connect.

185
00:34:16.699 --> 00:34:26.840
Art A: And they should immediately… be, removed from the registers of being PDP conformant.

186
00:34:27.380 --> 00:34:43.710
Art A: with a cure period for being able to re-register once things are in a stable state. So, it's, again, a continuous disclosure obligation versus, like, a continuous audit obligation, and that's really on the part of

187
00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:46.929
Art A: The, the company that's receiving status.

188
00:34:48.739 --> 00:34:55.779
Katariina Forsberg: Now, some people have asked whether this is moving too fast for something this big. Art, why this pace?

189
00:34:56.780 --> 00:34:59.909
Art A: Yeah, it is moving fast. I mean.

190
00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:09.800
Art A: Fundamentally, what it is, is that, we… need to move… And so, there's a…

191
00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:34.399
Art A: If you look at the regulatory landscape, a lot of the time is running out for implementing data portability in different countries. So, for example, we're working with the Dominican Republic right now, I'm heading there next week, to explore with them what it would look like to do a PDPP reference implementation, to allow data to move between government ministries and between ministries in the private sector. Why is that important for them? Well, they passed a law requiring this to happen, and I think

192
00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:39.600
Art A: They're looking at ways to implement this within a global standard at,

193
00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:59.360
Art A: at the pace in which their law requires. Similarly, we're heading to Korea at the end of the week, and Korea has their MyData regime, which is under law, that private sector needs to permit data portability by 2027. All of Korean private sector, and I don't think they have a technical implementation of that yet.

194
00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:07.719
Art A: So, we're coming up against, push pressures from different regimes that,

195
00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:22.409
Art A: without any changes to the law are requiring some implementation of data portability. The pull pressures are also coming up, insofar as data is now very closely tied to AI capabilities within countries and with institutions.

196
00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:31.319
Art A: At the same time, there is regulatory and, other risks associated with using data for AI technology.

197
00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:41.359
Art A: And we're seeing this in the EU, for example, where partnering with the EU Sandbox for AI Regulation to get an opinion on

198
00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:45.549
Art A: the extent to which a PDPP implementation on VARNA is…

199
00:36:45.550 --> 00:37:03.249
Art A: conformant with EU regulatory standards. So the pool factors here are related to AI, where people see it as a huge risk for companies to be ingesting data related to AI, and they want something in place. Now, we could certainly march slowly and not have the standard in place.

200
00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:12.420
Art A: But I think we're gonna miss the wave, and I think that right now, the demand is there. And so, where demand exists, I think it's important for us as a community to meet supply.

201
00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:27.630
Art A: So yes, this is fast. I would rather have several years of review, and a lot of academics look at this, but I'd rather get it out there in practice. The other thing I'll note is VARNA's been implementing, essentially, a PDPP conformant framework since 2024.

202
00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:34.740
Art A: And we've been doing it fairly successfully, and while I don't think that that locks the standard in, I think it offers

203
00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:47.170
Art A: a template for how something like this can be implemented at a global scale, and I do think that we're able to do it as long as we move really quickly on this. So, one thing that I will mention here is if any members have any…

204
00:37:47.240 --> 00:38:06.609
Art A: ideas or suggestions or, you know, must-haves for, things that should be implemented before we go live, please provide that comment. As Kat mentioned, the comment period for the standard closes on the 1st of October, so I would want, anything, any comments in by then.

205
00:38:08.390 --> 00:38:18.790
Katariina Forsberg: All right, so we are at the end of our questions here. So what… what else do we need people… what do we need help with right now, besides…

206
00:38:18.790 --> 00:38:28.079
Art A: We're definitely getting people to review the standard. So, PDPP.dev, review the standard, and also, if you align with the PDPP principles, show your support.

207
00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:48.080
Art A: I think that we run a small team here at VARNA, and we're all doing this on a voluntary basis outside of our work to try to make sure that this is a global standard for data portability. So if anybody also wants to be part of the core team, you're more than welcome to come. Kat's doing a fantastic job of bringing us all together. I would love to invite folks that are not

208
00:38:48.080 --> 00:39:04.789
Art A: part of the Varna community, part of the Linux community, or more broadly, to just come in and sit in on these meetings, but also any planning meetings that we're having, in relation to, kind of, moving this community along and getting it to grow. But yeah, that's pretty much it.

209
00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:15.120
Art A: And, yeah, very open to questions, you can reach Kat and I directly, and any comments on anything that we've raised today is something that we're very open to. Kat, anything else before we close?

210
00:39:15.590 --> 00:39:33.530
Katariina Forsberg: That is all. Thank you very much. Once again, the comments close on October 1st, so please add anything from today on GitHub or the mailing list. And our next call is October 8th, on the technical side with Tim, whom a lot of you have already met.

211
00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:37.070
Art A: Amazing. Thank you so much, Kat. Take care, guys. Bye.

212
00:39:37.070 --> 00:39:37.810
Katariina Forsberg: Bye.

